        "ai_gen_block_0029bb1_JLcCqp": {
          "type": "ai_gen_block_0029bb1",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "The GIA Diamond Colour Scale",
            "text": "<p>Founded in 1931, the Gemological Institute of America (GIA) is a non-profit organisation recognised for its work in gemmological research, education, analysis, and diamond grading. Its standards are used internationally to bring greater consistency and confidence to diamond assessment.</p><p>In the early 1950s, the GIA introduced its diamond colour grading system to replace the varied and often subjective language previously used to describe diamond colour. Earlier grading terms included letters such as A, B, and C, repeated A grades for exceptional stones, Arabic and Roman numerals, and descriptive phrases like “gem blue” or “blue white”.</p><p>To create a clearer and more neutral system, the GIA began its scale at D, a letter that had not already been strongly associated with the finest diamonds. Today, the GIA diamond colour scale includes 23 grades, divided into five categories:<br/><br/><strong>D – F: </strong>Colourless</p><p><strong>G – J: </strong>Near colourless</p><p><strong>K – M:</strong> Faint</p><p><strong>N – R:</strong> Very light</p><p><strong>S – Z: </strong>Light</p>",
            "heading_size_desktop": 30,
            "text_size_desktop": 18,
            "padding_left_desktop": 380,
            "padding_right_desktop": 380,
            "spacing_desktop": 40,
            "heading_size_mobile": 20,
            "text_size_mobile": 14,
            "padding_left_mobile": 40,
            "padding_right_mobile": 40,
            "spacing_mobile": 20,
            "text_alignment": "center",
            "heading_color": "#000000",
            "text_color": "#000000"
          },
          "blocks": {}
        }
      },
      "block_order": [
        "ai_gen_block_0029bb1_JLcCqp"
      ],
      "settings": {}
